Question 1: Partitioning

Step 1: Create the partitioned table and insert records

  SQL

  CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS prac;
  USE prac;

  CREATE TABLE employee (
     emp_id INT,
     emp_name VARCHAR(30),
     dept VARCHAR(20),
     salary INT
  )
  PARTITION BY RANGE (salary) (
     PARTITION p0 VALUES LESS THAN (30000),
     PARTITION p1 VALUES LESS THAN (50000),
     PARTITION p2 VALUES LESS THAN (70000),
     PARTITION p3 VALUES LESS THAN MAXVALUE
  );

  INSERT INTO employee VALUES
  (1,'Rahul','IT',25000),
  (2,'Amit','HR',45000),
  (3,'Sneha','IT',65000),
  (4,'Priya','Sales',85000),
  (5,'Karan','HR',30000),
  (6,'Neha','Sales',72000);

Step 2: Display records of each partition

  SQL

  SELECT * FROM employee PARTITION (p0);
  SELECT * FROM employee PARTITION (p1);
  SELECT * FROM employee PARTITION (p2);
  SELECT * FROM employee PARTITION (p3);

  SELECT PARTITION_NAME, PARTITION_METHOD, PARTITION_DESCRIPTION, TABLE_ROWS
  FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.PARTITIONS
  WHERE TABLE_SCHEMA = 'prac' AND TABLE_NAME = 'employee';

Step 3: DML operations (UPDATE and DELETE)

  SQL

  UPDATE employee SET salary = 55000 WHERE emp_id = 1;
  UPDATE employee SET dept = 'HR' WHERE emp_id = 3;
  DELETE FROM employee WHERE emp_id = 5;

Step 4: Display each partition again

  SQL

  SELECT * FROM employee PARTITION (p0);
  SELECT * FROM employee PARTITION (p1);
  SELECT * FROM employee PARTITION (p2);
  SELECT * FROM employee PARTITION (p3);

Partition-wise data storage
 • Partitioning: one logical table is split into smaller physical parts called partitions, based on
   a partition key column (here salary).
 • Range partitioning: each partition holds rows within a range of the key. Salary below
   30000 goes to p0, 30000 to 49999 to p1, 50000 to 69999 to p2, 70000 and above to p3.
 • Hash partitioning: MySQL computes (key value MOD number of partitions) and the result
   decides the partition, so rows spread evenly.
 • Working: on INSERT, MySQL stores the row in the matching partition automatically; on
   UPDATE of the key, the row moves to the new partition. A query that filters on the key reads
   only the needed partition (partition pruning), so retrieval is faster.

Question 2: Distributed Transactions

Distributed transaction
A distributed transaction is a transaction that updates data in more than one database (or site)
and must still behave as a single unit: either all the updates are saved or none of them
(atomicity). The seat is reserved in the Booking database and the payment is recorded in the
Payment database. Either both happen or neither.

Example: Online ticket booking across two databases (Booking and Payment)

  SQL

  CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS Booking;
  CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS Payment;

  CREATE TABLE Booking.seat (seat_no INT, status VARCHAR(10), user_id INT);
  CREATE TABLE Payment.payment (pay_id INT, user_id INT, amount INT, status VARCHAR(10));

  INSERT INTO Booking.seat VALUES (12,'FREE',NULL);

  START TRANSACTION;
  UPDATE Booking.seat SET status = 'BOOKED', user_id = 101 WHERE seat_no = 12;
  INSERT INTO Payment.payment VALUES (5001,101,450,'PAID');
  SELECT * FROM Booking.seat;
  SELECT * FROM Payment.payment;
  COMMIT;

  SELECT * FROM Booking.seat;
  SELECT * FROM Payment.payment;

Purpose of COMMIT and ROLLBACK
 • COMMIT: saves all the changes of the transaction permanently in all the databases. Used
   when every step of the transaction succeeds. The database moves to a new consistent state
   and both databases show the completed work.
 • ROLLBACK: cancels all the changes of the transaction in all the databases. Used when any
   step fails or the user cancels. The database returns to the state before START
   TRANSACTION, so no half-done work remains and data stays consistent.

Why transaction recovery is required
1. To ensure Atomicity. A transaction must be completed fully or not at all. If a failure occurs
   after some operations, the completed operations must be undone (ROLLBACK).
2. To handle different types of failures. Transactions can fail due to a system crash, power
   failure, disk failure, network failure between the databases, deadlock or logical errors. The
   DBMS must recover from all of them.
3. To maintain Consistency of the database. Partial updates leave the databases in an
   incorrect state, for example money debited in one database but not credited in the other.
   Recovery brings them back to a consistent state.

4. To ensure Durability. Once a transaction is committed, its changes must remain
   permanent even if the system crashes immediately afterwards. Recovery uses the log file to
   redo committed transactions.
5. To avoid wrong data being used by other transactions. If a failed transaction's
   uncommitted changes remain, other transactions may read incorrect values. Recovery
   removes these changes.

Conclusion: Transaction recovery protects the ACID properties and keeps the databases
correct and reliable after any failure.

